    "title": "Primewise Persistent Homology Predicts Rank Jumps in Elliptic Curve Quadratic Tw",
    "description": "Conjecture: Let E/Q be a non-CM elliptic curve. For each squarefree integer d and each good prime p not dividing 2dN_E, form a finite filtered complex K_p(E_d) functorially from the reduction of the quadratic twist E_d over F_p, using only the Frobenius action data on E_d(F_p) and its low-order isogeny graph neighborhood. Define the aggregated primewise persistence profile P_X(E_d) from primes p <= X. Then there exists an explicit statistic S_X(E_d), computable from P_X(E_d), such that as X -> infinity it distinguishes with positive asymptotic bias between twists of analytic rank 0 and twists of analytic rank >= 2 in any one-parameter quadratic twist family of E. Test: Fix E and compute K_p(E_d) for many squarefree d up to a height bound and primes p <= X; compare the classifier S_X(E_d) against ranks known from L-function computations. The conjecture is supported if prediction accuracy stays uniformly above random and improves with X across multiple families, and refuted if no functorial construction in the proposed class beats random guessing asymptotically. Impact: This would provide a new topological probe of Birch\u2013Swinnerton-Dyer phenomena, potentially revealing hidden geometric structure in rank variation and suggesting new invariants for arithmetic statistics of elliptic curves.",
